ld.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c5ae160-d78c-57fc-8666-ed9ed6778702" :question/math-academy-id "q-65828" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8166bb-3a9d-5808-af53-a18ca99920f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433b1dec-1b44-57e1-96ac-8fad713d4cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333616} {:answer/id #uuid "7b34dc7d-d206-5607-b7f9-5a235ecefe4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334093} {:answer/id #uuid "8e293bba-0f89-5b76-9798-3cf43c90f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334258} {:answer/id #uuid "96e39967-aa65-59d7-95f2-7942c903b86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334333} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8f5f08-15e6-5a0d-8cae-0349318ef2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe8f5f08-15e6-5a0d-8cae-0349318ef2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335229} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ad33e5d-8257-569d-ad73-09689f260100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc4aa73-a240-5ce1-93bd-0fb1716e68ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-65848" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=b\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $b?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9e80b8-56ad-53a3-b026-16e3818bf6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333243} {:answer/id #uuid "3346694f-e05f-5c9a-8364-9e84121ce996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333463} {:answer/id #uuid "398fced3-5d50-539a-85a7-4f195276b87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333528} {:answer/id #uuid "8203193a-3ef1-55a8-9573-da4a680393e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334147} {:answer/id #uuid "85c2c835-ab1a-5c4f-8204-3e5c80081a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9e80b8-56ad-53a3-b026-16e3818bf6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333243} :answer-field/id #uuid "49903033-3da4-57d6-9ad8-8e12caa8bf6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b5b8276-6c04-5334-a46c-67373ce92195" :question/math-academy-id "q-65851" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=1$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada6e26-3e25-5024-8918-75ece9936f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109916} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c31b7-948e-5ad7-b087-0fe89630298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109917} {:answer/id #uuid "4c34414e-2009-5cf3-b56c-4690e524521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109918} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4d65ad-f84e-57b5-af67-61b03c8dcd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109919} {:answer/id #uuid "793ba2fd-9d15-5423-bdbe-c7924d65df92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109920}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b398f04b-0e66-5616-855b-c595b649c5d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bfad6c-2814-5c54-aba4-2b500b7ae26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403212} {:answer/id #uuid "206105e2-44d4-5574-89ca-ddf2279a77dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403213} {:answer/id #uuid "d1df0086-7f48-50cb-8ceb-18066bc865de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403214} {:answer/id #uuid "29558d43-c76b-586a-9425-52db91027e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403215} {:answer/id #uuid "c6000078-e4e1-5811-8829-82307d46879b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bfad6c-2814-5c54-aba4-2b500b7ae26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403212} :answer-field/id #uuid "389f0e82-b83e-5348-b35b-7ad0561fa28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7680c22b-fa61-58ad-8d65-6ff8999855b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-65947" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b113f0a9-895a-56a3-8759-b6c2959ff8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109922} {:answer/id #uuid "bf303d45-b0dd-5d6d-a581-736ceb085d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109923} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a086e3-1d0d-522d-a5d7-a925c56f89cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109924} {:answer/id #uuid "08f55e75-52ef-560e-b53a-8c9c154bd4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109925} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdf6e3b-0b34-539c-933c-fbf55734dae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109926}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c79a2455-4317-550b-bb8d-c5b6422cc769" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0415b469-b139-5e1a-a412-34227a3897af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403218} {:answer/id #uuid "23feee12-2b3b-5956-a66b-2f5a51f37208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403219} {:answer/id #uuid "d3754b87-9f75-54d9-9a94-d542f1aaa946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403220} {:answer/id #uuid "4215ac44-f44e-59a5-8b63-fb6307287e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403221} {:answer/id #uuid "46e2afd4-b53a-541a-99a5-3d5382aea9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0415b469-b139-5e1a-a412-34227a3897af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403218} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f631db-f510-51e8-a456-9f86ddcaf626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b81fb291-176e-5b1d-97d6-f57c92461362" :question/math-academy-id "q-65990" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\nII. $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f640cb-1d0e-55cf-82bf-2bc2df4f2cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403225} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4fdfad-f0bb-539a-b3dd-5135ecf4149a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403226} {:answer/id #uuid "de09ab34-6244-5de9-adc0-7d2808c13ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403227} {:answer/id #uuid "a347e69f-18e6-5a4a-896e-70ebdae9cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403228} {:answer/id #uuid "87a5d244-0e82-5176-9600-2dd50bb45515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de09ab34-6244-5de9-adc0-7d2808c13ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403227} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca79eb9-6a7d-56ad-a7b2-6517a75087f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28fa7313-ebe9-5114-aa43-a48667cbf78a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66005" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2860f091-989b-5acf-bed7-92b08075eb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333362} {:answer/id #uuid "5040b834-24a3-5df1-a20e-564c56b47beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333721} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59ee0e-58fc-5a3d-949d-14b9d219ff2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333857} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbc418e-a7e2-54c0-9ca4-dfc253bf86d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} {:answer/id #uuid "b17cc870-94e7-5614-a58c-ba7c33e83987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b17cc870-94e7-5614-a58c-ba7c33e83987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334547} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f40443f-a936-5a8c-8318-c86ccbab6f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "168eeab0-e1e9-5e07-9123-192a4db7e669" :question/math-academy-id "q-66027" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{3})}=6\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c1bf3-8a2c-597a-9691-8aa91eb60ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333201} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9155b-c99f-5edf-bcf7-47ee202d8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334262} {:answer/id #uuid "95b7052b-37ce-5396-bb77-088b9296066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{5}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334319} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f67f59-e931-5aaa-9a96-f02861af4dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334746} {:answer/id #uuid "d20dc049-4ebe-592f-807b-f45ee2cbfd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20dc049-4ebe-592f-807b-f45ee2cbfd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334823} :answer-field/id #uuid "91da3a41-f121-5ac4-8e56-5b5d087a0e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebf23ba9-8897-5181-bd95-1d3aa5290158" :question/math-academy-id "q-66062" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18946078-156b-530c-a4de-ec62bf8e85c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333210} {:answer/id #uuid "20777da5-0984-5da1-8bf7-28aefc2a0507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333281} {:answer/id #uuid "34f22782-537d-53ce-82d9-e53ddc550fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333473} {:answer/id #uuid "38debac9-c2a9-5848-9b8a-69647498387b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333517} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc967c8-96e4-5016-ad2d-19c23ad22677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc967c8-96e4-5016-ad2d-19c23ad22677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334105} :answer-field/id #uuid "af8ca650-d540-5a79-83d8-42636f12d580" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1cc5b07-00f4-5f7e-8eb9-17930a833879" :question/math-academy-id "q-66074" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057c25b9-7a52-5c3a-8fe5-7432d12255fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333034} {:answer/id #uuid "0af09b2d-a373-5fdf-86f6-f7a7becd248f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{4\\pi }{3}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333090} {:answer/id #uuid "1740e99f-4eaa-5252-b012-95911470c710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333197} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e75ba5-2115-5855-bdca-002c752777fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{5}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334689} {:answer/id #uuid "fa275f70-eacd-54a0-870a-69bc857ab05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057c25b9-7a52-5c3a-8fe5-7432d12255fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333034} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b415ba-2ef7-53a9-9318-6f70b6830e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0563777-3978-5dfe-ad52-2564ef2dce5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66076" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059a85b1-1331-54f5-ac5d-05e971afc5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333036} {:answer/id #uuid "15d77f8b-c447-5111-9560-6dcbd69ff783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333185} {:answer/id #uuid "4a146762-9914-5bd6-b982-ebcd792b55d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333671} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bc02e9-b519-5201-b7c7-46b7ba73d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334672} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a75991-364b-57be-9860-17d3bb69f242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d77f8b-c447-5111-9560-6dcbd69ff783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333185} :answer-field/id #uuid "18a140b5-4f5c-588e-bf4c-bb4cc3b31fbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdecb3c9-ad89-5b16-8c08-22bbffb1f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-66083" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}$ and adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}$ at the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bd8c6d8-0b11-5735-88a1-9b12e6315401" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e9e8cc-7e2c-5b9c-8895-6a0d01a94483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333213} {:answer/id #uuid "2550874f-8ee9-5a21-ad79-124324b76991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡({3}^{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333335} {:answer/id #uuid "602373f3-a4ef-5989-b10a-66c28e72546b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}ln⁡3+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333867} {:answer/id #uuid "93360ddb-2f30-5402-b6bd-6562c3c2c6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334300} {:answer/id #uuid "94f01e4b-399e-55ad-bf45-f119b26a99ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18e9e8cc-7e2c-5b9c-8895-6a0d01a94483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333213} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7cb00b-1c03-5bd1-8af9-27f6fe69ec11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "709a586a-cd76-5969-9f50-4116711ad7ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-66139" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7d8b31-ae04-5df6-aeb1-961f777e33b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-108,\\,x=10\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403232} {:answer/id #uuid "3477ce1f-9124-5c8e-aee7-0c19aaa37d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-108,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403233} {:answer/id #uuid "516e48e9-f368-58d1-b1c7-a413f32276d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403234} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9c6c12-d688-5f40-9928-9159b2b770a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=10\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403235} {:answer/id #uuid "a06bc635-fc47-5897-92f1-2184d35dc008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "516e48e9-f368-58d1-b1c7-a413f32276d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403234} :answer-field/id #uuid "042ad804-2249-5399-b919-45947b7e0d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f70143e0-3f9c-51d2-946d-0c2579968816" :question/math-academy-id "q-66435" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}+16x+164=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+164 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+16x & =-164\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x & =-164 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}-{8}^{2} & =-164 \\\\ (x+8)^{2}-64 & =-164 \\\\ (x+8)^{2} & =-100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8,\\,q=-100,$ and $p+q=-92.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8)^{2} & =-100 \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-100} \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm 10\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-8+10\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-8-10\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046ec56a-2154-55dd-81a9-19e9359e8a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403239} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe7f7f0-cfc0-5858-9600-b3b2c717f329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403240} {:answer/id #uuid "813d4ece-3add-5aa3-95e4-73b6c64ea24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403241} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbfd34e-4c5f-5bda-b5d4-833bc6a12f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403242} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc0a540-ce92-5907-a74f-b7d9a91e069f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046ec56a-2154-55dd-81a9-19e9359e8a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403239} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ad16e3-732f-5dec-b28a-7752a9983d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b9771ce-36d1-5cb6-a7ee-84e40d892ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-66508" :question/problem "![](images/23/239490fd3be930412d4f67ff89244607a5019d91f201bc4e579410b5610a18ed.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/c7/c728d173c13a1e7b3bcad0e9f0284c97f2ca075c795dcb1f38ed086c19cc0504.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I  and IV show one-to-one functions. This means that  these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8c6b41-dda4-5b72-b40c-3e85cba087db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403246} {:answer/id #uuid "8c44c0b8-a324-51aa-8160-2529aa798669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403247} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ff6185-1935-54af-a0dd-0d7567723234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403248} {:answer/id #uuid "22145362-fe93-590d-8788-5b69cf80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403249} {:answer/id #uuid "5297b063-4a7c-5a1f-aec3-8f8e6212d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5297b063-4a7c-5a1f-aec3-8f8e6212d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403250} :answer-field/id #uuid "6127ad12-ca4b-5bf9-9f70-8f5bdf2f9c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d080acd-916c-5650-8a33-fa75e4ad9891" :question/math-academy-id "q-66509" :question/problem "![](images/be/be5b7625ad6f540f6769b121f52ccad69b669dbf764fc3bb68f89c99f066f145.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7d94de67ceeb0c4aaa0988c117fce7f15242c2f3d223eecd8f0eb77135d7e.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that  the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a79b73a-03f6-5533-acf7-b5a1df8567d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403253} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1c26d9-e125-518f-bb45-e4c39ea28969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403254} {:answer/id #uuid "168d57eb-da99-5a01-9c14-4caceb4907e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403255} {:answer/id #uuid "a58e69ca-7b57-5342-91cd-29668910ad83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403256} {:answer/id #uuid "18db7e36-ba98-517a-818f-12aea00ad73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58e69ca-7b57-5342-91cd-29668910ad83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403256} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff70fd34-fe3d-556f-9676-12869b9e2163" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e687e18c-9edd-57ea-b75b-3cb3764012f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-66514" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f65062d48189401ba22b1b08637d187eed532d5f93dca7663c2e80743510d5d7.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/cc/cc31f6f291058c7890a9210d5ebfac21cdd961c74c3db5e080d7fca334102b53.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} {:answer/id #uuid "2f451149-34e3-5874-ac22-67e5f7f806cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333429} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1f9842-ca0a-55db-bcb6-06735b728dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x{)}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334496} {:answer/id #uuid "b1df2cc8-3e8e-59c7-a92f-f4f573b06622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334551} {:answer/id #uuid "d848947b-033e-583b-9707-793c5c4bf6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(4+6x{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} :answer-field/id #uuid "564e943b-61e3-5dd9-b71b-ad37ff586952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f5104a-6ff3-54f4-aa5c-62ed37ead198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333080} {:answer/id #uuid "63585c27-7a3a-5092-97b9-8b6f85d39e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333888} {:answer/id #uuid "73594949-931f-5c38-acef-ed3ae383d30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c1fdd-bd1b-547b-a00c-f61e00401b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334431} {:answer/id #uuid "fac57336-3737-5816-be0e-c9b839b24604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73594949-931f-5c38-acef-ed3ae383d30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034} :answer-field/id #uuid "73908950-ca6a-51b9-b8ec-b5131be094bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd590154-6c93-5483-8fea-428e3503f4be" :question/math-academy-id "q-66603" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then what is $4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3 & =4(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f34d47e-e833-592c-bec3-bc39b0793775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395920} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9ab566-ebba-5dfd-86b0-6349eece69a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395921} {:answer/id #uuid "9a355765-0b97-5a42-8de4-b0b60654f204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395922} {:answer/id #uuid "a0808324-6585-5b82-a886-35c16e8f895c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395923} {:answer/id #uuid "14d0b756-faff-5e66-b7a4-e750eb38e0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f34d47e-e833-592c-bec3-bc39b0793775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395920} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2ff532-22a4-53e5-b06b-7bb83b2a55a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b33211c6-2184-5991-90d3-02829e541c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6664" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-8x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{3}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(3)$ is a factor of $-3$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2116cd79-44ea-53c6-889d-f1682fbd66e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333285} {:answer/id #uuid "3d232036-8fad-5550-abc3-00107d785ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333565} {:answer/id #uuid "71502cbf-4578-5c31-b861-904cd30b4751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334019} {:answer/id #uuid "78d38142-d9f0-58b7-a882-9c15bbb0ea41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334072} {:answer/id #uuid "b85f9153-ad14-57e3-93d0-fc16c152374a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85f9153-ad14-57e3-93d0-fc16c152374a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334605} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a2344e-da17-5c35-be6d-dd0a9ebe6076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f07d7f91-d30a-5dce-8cae-ea7d095128ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-66667" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then what is $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1=5\\sqrt{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057e6ce9-267d-57cc-ae69-ad84b7ee42ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333035} {:answer/id #uuid "2670d583-ba23-509d-984a-336387ded173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333342} {:answer/id #uuid "818226b6-2143-5b12-bb7a-35ba69eb59b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334139} {:answer/id #uuid "81f500aa-4f70-5107-844f-19fa4e38c1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334145} {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363} :answer-field/id #uuid "66899f0d-563e-5ed9-a6cb-77e4a5b3f25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ae354c-e4a4-50d9-b820-8ba146650b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-66669" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,5)$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02418c3-4c04-57b8-8f10-18858b1759ab" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a380137-f59b-5306-a7a0-f3353de4ed0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333229} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0d53db-4f56-5d47-834f-5d0a01b3b218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334645} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ecd3ad-6a48-51e0-8fbe-3683fafde9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334700} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d7434a-a12d-54d8-b9e6-b5586d783803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334833} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f9298f-edad-54b0-8f2a-c96bc8cdcdd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf0d53db-4f56-5d47-834f-5d0a01b3b218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334645} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed8dda0-5ccd-57b5-b04b-85458a4c39d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93cbc51f-bbe5-5350-b365-2efa0de8e1c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-66675" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ for some $x$, then find the value of $\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\pi )-4.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-1$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x=cot⁡(x+(-1)\\cdot \\pi )=cot⁡(x-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{3},$ then $cot⁡(x-\\pi )=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\pi )-4 & =\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3})-4 \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =3-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} {:answer/id #uuid "253331ca-df9d-523a-8811-2487a95d3272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333334} {:answer/id #uuid "63786589-d496-56b9-bccb-1a7b14aff44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333890} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfaecb8-b49e-5068-aef0-3b70aa437c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334362} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2e9670-55bb-542c-a3ec-ea63c48da4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd33408-f473-5e92-b602-88c8f1b4211b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb9d3e2-6b9a-5fb3-b64e-0b7d921759d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-66679" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/b0/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6034f8d-aa19-5323-b77c-9c3e9840bc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082278} {:answer/id #uuid "dda9558b-7186-57d5-a556-24b82b6fe6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082279} {:answer/id #uuid "fa067bc6-591d-5442-89b0-4bb61deffa95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082280} {:answer/id #uuid "29a21594-229a-524b-b19a-e19944ed8938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082281} {:answer/id #uuid "79b3ab26-2ad0-508c-bbd0-cb7f4dcb206e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082282}] :answer-field/id #uuid "abbccfce-f350-5b66-8f1c-7c3548b3e2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd1cd6a-285c-548a-adba-d82870244265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395926} {:answer/id #uuid "131a7494-8f01-55ba-92bc-b15e88fd9846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395927} {:answer/id #uuid "269d0de3-6a01-5def-89db-3dc958c2558c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395928} {:answer/id #uuid "093668c9-e9ba-5cdb-99a9-79d2c36efe70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395929} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f49533-db03-5f90-9f04-b99e950b449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f49533-db03-5f90-9f04-b99e950b449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395930} :answer-field/id #uuid "754d2cb4-bc72-56f5-9aae-e0a939de763c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc161f0-8951-58a5-87a8-35a7ed39116e" :question/math-academy-id "q-6671" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-10x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-6$ does not take the above form. The value $-6$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{6}{1},$ and the numerator $(6)$ is not a factor of $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d49c863-bcca-5003-b60b-2402ac1bbc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396689} {:answer/id #uuid "372a1527-fcb7-5e97-9214-ceab892980ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396690} {:answer/id #uuid "89d11ef2-0ebb-56ef-85f5-eff24a1c20ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396691} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396692} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd9f23-0f52-529d-9d3d-4b581fbbb61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396692} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb55a4d3-ef9d-5ba1-bc8d-64b5b8502eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8061fba9-94fb-59ab-bb67-c981e34d25e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66724" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\nIII. $x\\in [-5,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d3/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d39eb8e-2acf-5989-93c9-5706e83e7f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334106} {:answer/id #uuid "86eabbba-0a20-58a9-b168-1143669ceead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334184} {:answer/id #uuid "e1269f37-8751-57fd-a7c9-4c6a8b91e67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334948} {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} {:answer/id #uuid "e693a8f1-2c29-5ee8-9bb7-6a65568289ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d2f2b9-b380-5289-b8eb-9732e9ebb27b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02e022db-b323-5e31-af64-39f79b6a6465" :question/math-academy-id "q-66725" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/aa/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ccd7d-3b34-57fa-9756-01a55bf6d389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333408} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4a0865-5f57-5aac-9e60-dbe69aaa7c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333415} {:answer/id #uuid "db70806a-1a03-5f87-9afd-163b0c228e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334908} {:answer/id #uuid "fce2b562-b937-5bdf-944f-df98ec166dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f3f896-e079-54b5-988f-9af076e4c242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ee60b09-63e9-5076-abae-7337fc233de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-66729" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-1|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-1|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-1|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-1|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-1|-2.$\n\n![](images/75/753840da17642582f9dbc0debc18f1fa3efcbbb11142894c1dd823ad6c0c630a.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/15/15f1c797cbddd2ac8e2e9c5be72f842e7d1accfc5f619af57b1f35a27e1015c0.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a1/a100489b49da7b06c98783b1b1c4ec0de9d891fc2b22f4b36b1a0ac2d54fc4f3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223c177a-d9d7-56cd-ad4a-3477d50d4c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333296} {:answer/id #uuid "323a913a-973d-59e1-bf67-21fa4537869b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333454} {:answer/id #uuid "6503bcd7-e33d-594d-9276-3205ef82e188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333907} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f071d-aa78-50ae-b1ac-3a53253f9868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334620} {:answer/id #uuid "d5be51db-4b95-5f18-bdba-64d3e9539848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f071d-aa78-50ae-b1ac-3a53253f9868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334620} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f34fa5-49b8-5d1e-8a10-c2a061a8bc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8c6efa-1e45-5ac2-8d8a-2e0aeaebd494" :question/math-academy-id "q-66863" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where ${4}^{x+4}={16}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $16$ as ${4}^{2},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+4} & ={16}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={({4}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={4}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d8d4f1-069f-5999-90cd-de6a076c1b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333165} {:answer/id #uuid "18fcade8-3d85-57f2-9926-025f7f6bda40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333215} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c31d51-ffad-5ede-a608-d0a37966955d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334728} {:answer/id #uuid "ded55b8b-cc37-5917-a902-1f995ea97dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334930} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f1e514-ed0c-50dc-9391-5f7fc7b497c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d8d4f1-069f-5999-90cd-de6a076c1b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333165} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf67bf2-60e8-5cd7-b3c5-b039e26df66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb40fa82-caf5-5c22-a8e0-5ae171e1b21d" :question/math-academy-id "q-66895" :question/problem "If ${81}^{3m}={27}^{m+5}$ then $m=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $81={3}^{4}$ and $27={3}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{4})}^{3m}={({3}^{3})}^{m+5}\\,\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{12m} & ={3}^{3(m+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12m & =3(m+5) \\\\ 12m & =3m+15 \\\\ 9m & =15 \\\\ m & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023d00f0-da47-5394-ae60-c245b8db80c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡3}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333008} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0a0f48-9424-5652-a7e7-eb0fd57d5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333989} {:answer/id #uuid "b328c212-2782-537f-811b-a0f14c00e543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334562} {:answer/id #uuid "c41479bc-c413-5965-9e33-9fcecff3e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334691} {:answer/id #uuid "c56d2556-b830-55ee-b819-6acd7ca640c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c41479bc-c413-5965-9e33-9fcecff3e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334691} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4b2c7f-91fb-5e15-9837-4a5d33297bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fbdd794-6943-5168-9b56-03ebeeb88547" :question/math-academy-id "q-66896" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-1}={3}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c39c05-6b09-5e35-b268-3f6a25a6781f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333654} {:answer/id #uuid "77ecf205-2c33-5276-ab7f-28d701a1eb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334066} {:answer/id #uuid "c6662232-1ea2-573b-89f4-2a0750e58b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334713} {:answer/id #uuid "e45aeee8-8186-5ebc-a6a2-79d46bf35372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334980} {:answer/id #uuid "fbea0bae-5ed2-5f44-b4c1-67abd6116d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47c39c05-6b09-5e35-b268-3f6a25a6781f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333654} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ed643b-742a-5c50-ac84-d78f4999d380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "597c9977-5e56-59c3-a533-0b9a014416da" :question/math-academy-id "q-66899" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{y+1}={3}^{2y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1} & ={3}^{2y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\ny+1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2y})\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\ny+1=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-y & =1 \\\\ y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ab7be2-dda3-5ed1-ac99-09c91d8ee0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.277" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333224} {:answer/id #uuid "30349d58-fc74-5b3b-bdfe-f35ac2999f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.277" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333435} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2a8989-51b3-5e13-ad5b-1afbc9a8f5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.727" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333990} {:answer/id #uuid "8324c711-5a4c-5526-bb63-c83f0d9eb296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334159} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa9c4c-5512-58eb-91d1-4c102e05372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30349d58-fc74-5b3b-bdfe-f35ac2999f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.277" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333435} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff74f9c-19ae-5a3d-b268-bbf4ae9475d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "773f6463-e638-5263-aed4-c3fe89e22103" :question/math-academy-id "q-66902" :question/problem "Given that ${e}^{-5z}-6=-2,$ find the value of $z$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5z}-6 & =-2 \\\\ {e}^{-5z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5z} & =4 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{-5z}) & =ln⁡(4) \\\\ -5z & =ln⁡(4) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{5}ln⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-\\frac{1}{5}ln⁡(4)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nz≈-0.277\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b529884-636b-5961-b3ab-0b1ffd6fc529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333237} {:answer/id #uuid "3af51505-3b7e-5a78-871c-fa0ebc86c991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333542} {:answer/id #uuid "3b94483d-a6bc-564f-bce8-4fcd125fc010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333553} {:answer/id #uuid "61e76b62-2870-51c9-8f98-a87d757ce7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333880} {:answer/id #uuid "7941a635-4aeb-5e3f-89e5-690e66c52c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61e76b62-2870-51c9-8f98-a87d757ce7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333880} :answer-field/id #uuid "87538856-f739-5dc3-ac6c-bbd1fe9a60af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18d9fccc-5763-5f9d-ba85-bde34b867636" :question/math-academy-id "q-67070" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n1+3+5+…=81\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=81$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=1.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2+2N-2) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N) \\\\ 81 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=9$ terms."}]}
